ottoms, Jackson hit campaign trail", "source": "Atlanta News First", "published": "2026-09-19T07:00:00+00:00", "url": "https://news.google.?oc=5"}, {"title": "New poll shows Jackson edging out Bottoms in Ga. governor’s race", "source": "WRDW", "published": "2026-09-17T07:00:00+00:00", "url": "https://news.google.?oc=5"}], "model": {"prior_margin": 1.93, "prior_sd": 5.34, "poll_margin": -0.93, "poll_sd": 5.03, "poll_weight": 0.53, "fundamentals": {"margin": 1.82, "lean": -0.75, "environment": 4.15, "incumbency": 0.0, "money": -1.58}, "rating_margin": 2.0, "sigma_race": 5.2, "sigma_total": 5.63, "baseline_margin": 0.42, "analyst_adjustment": -1.42, "key_factors": ["Rick Jackson's roughly 20-to-1 fundraising edge and a large Democratic ad-spending deficit", "September polls from a mix of pollsters show Jackson narrowly ahead by 1-3 points", "A Democratic-friendly national environment and Georgia's near-even lean keep Bottoms close", "Open seat with a tossup consensus among raters; markets price Bottoms around 45-47%"], "rationale": "Every poll taken since mid-September shows Jackson ahead by one to three points, and his enormous financial advantage is shaping the air war into early voting, while a favorable environment for Democrats and Georgia's even partisan balance keep Bottoms within striking distance. Markets and most raters treat this as a coin flip with a slight Republican tilt.", "watch": "A late infusion of outside money for Bottoms, or unusually high turnout in metro Atlanta, could erase Jackson's slim edge.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Brian Kemp (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Georgia's governor's race is a genuine tossup to succeed term-limited Republican Brian Kemp. Democrat Keisha Lance Bottoms, the former Atlanta mayor, faces Republican Rick Jackson in a contest most raters call a tossup, though two rate Democrats slightly ahead. Polling since mid-September shows Jackson narrowly ahead, with margins between one and three points, while prediction markets give Bottoms roughly a 45 to 47 percent chance of winning.\n\nThe defining feature is money: Jackson has raised about $119 million to Bottoms' $5.8 million, and news reports describe a staggering Democratic advertising deficit heading into early voting. A favorable national environment for Democrats and Georgia's near-even partisan lean keep the race tight, but the spending gap and the recent polling trend give Jackson a slim edge. Expect a margin of roughly a point, with real room for movement if the air war or turnout shifts.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.6414, "margin": 1.75}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.5561, "margin": 0.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.5612, "margin": 0.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.5894, "margin": 0.8}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.5413, "margin": 0.8}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.5396, "margin": 0.8}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.5736, "margin": 0.8}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.463, "margin": -0.7}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.5513, "margin": 0.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.5602, "margin": 0.5}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.4361, "margin": -1.0}]}